ize d, where N/4<d<N/3.\n\nThe following example shows that sometimes the height is greater than γ(G)/2, so we cannot conclude the CSS\n\nconjecture without computing β(G) explicitly.\n\nExample 10 Let N =14 and A ={1,2,8,9}⊂(Z/14 Z)∗. Then N/4<d<N/3. Since 0 is not in A,2 A\n\nand 3 A, G =Cay(Z/14 Z,E A)is a triangle-free digraph. We have h 14(1,2,8,9)=20 and γ(G)=35. Thus\n\nh 14(1,2,8,9)>γ(G)/2.\n\nAcknowledgment\n\nThis work grows out of the second author’s master thesis at Chulalongkorn University written under the direc-\n\ntion of the ﬁrst author to which the second author expresses his gratitude. The authors would like to thank the\n\nreferees for valuable comments and suggestions which improved the paper.\n\nReferences\n\nBatson, J. (2008). Nathanson heights in ﬁnite vector spaces. J. Number Theory, 128, no.9, 2616-2633.\n\nChudnovsky, M., Seymour, P. & Sullivan, B. D. (2007). Cycles in dense digraphs. arXiv:math.CO/0702147.\n\nHamidoune, Y. O. (1981). An application of connectivity theory in graphs to factorizations of elements in\n\ngroups. European J. Combin., 2, no. 4, 349-355.\n\nNathanson, M. B. (2006). The Caccetta-H¨\n\naggkvist conjecture and additive number theory. arXiv:math.CO/0603469.\n\nNathanson, M. B. & Sullivan, B. D. (2007). Heights in ﬁnite projective space, and a problem on directed\n\ngraphs. arXiv: math.CO/0703418.\n\n¢www.ccsenet.org/jmr 7\n\nV ol. 1, No. 1 ISSN: 1916-9795\n\nThe Set R∗and Its Properties\n\nShengwu Zhu\n\nNorthwest University\n\nNo. 36 Xinmin street, Xincheng District Xi’an 710004, China\n\nTel: 86-29-8197-8655 E-mail: zhufang04@lzu.cn\n\nAbstract\n\nIn this paper, we shall give a set R∗and indicate its properties, and thus, some abnormal results, such as the\n\nlimit number may be successor, the natural number may be transﬁnite, the inﬁnite set can not be equipotent to\n\nits proper subset etc., will be obtained.\n\nKeywords: Remainder matrix, Axis number, Double-direction induction\n\n1. On R(n)\n\nAssume the ordered set formed by front (n+1) prime numbers on the number axis (We refer only to the non-\n\nnegative integers on right of number axis) in accordance with natural order be\n\nD n={d 0,d 1,d 2,···,d n}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics, Probability and Computing (https://ftp.math.utah.edu/pub/tex/bib/toc/combinprobabcomput.html)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3.1 years ago; Volume 17, Number 5, September, 2008 ...                      Carlos Vinuesa   $ B_2 [g] $ Sets and a Conjecture of ... Hamidoune   A Weighted Generalization of Gao's $ n +\nM. Koh   Bounds For The Real Zeros of Chromatic\n                                      Polynomials  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 749--759\n                    Martin Dyer and   \n            Leslie Ann Goldberg and   \n                        Mark Jerrum   Dobrushin Conditions and Systematic Scan 761--779\n               Ohad N. Feldheim and   \n                Michael Krivelevich   Winning Fast in Sparse Graph\n                                      Construction Games . . . . . . . . . . . 781--791\n                 Yahya O. Hamidoune   A Weighted Generalization of Gao's $ n +\n                                      D - 1 $ Theorem  . . . . . . . . . . . . 793--798\n                      Svante Janson   Individual Displacements in Hashing with\n                                      Coalesced Chains . . . . . . . . . . . . 799--814\n             Ken-Ichi Kawarabayashi   A Weakening of the Odd Hadwiger's\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch results for Abstract analysis (https://www.cambridge.org/core/browse-subjects/mathematics/abstract-analysis/listing?pageNum=790)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * ### On Weighted Sequence Sums\n\n  *   * Yahya Ould Hamidoune ...     12 September 2008, pp.\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n      *     * Export citation\n  * View abstract\n\nA graph is vertex-transitive (edge-transitive) if its automorphism group acts transitively on the vertices (edges, resp.). The expansion rate of a subset S of the vertex set is the quotient e(S):= |∂(S)|/|S|, where ∂(S) denotes the set of vertices not in S but adjacent to some vertex in S. Improving and extending previous results of Aldous and Babai, we give very simple proofs of the following results. Let X be a (finite or infinite) vertex-transitive graph and let S be a finite subset of the vertices. If X is finite, we also assume |S| ≤|V(X)/2. Let d be the diameter of S in the metric induced by X. Then e(S) ≥1/(d + 1); and e(S) ≥ 2/(d +2) if X is finite and d is less than the diameter of X. If X is edge-transitive then |δ(S)|/|S| ≥ r/(2d), where ∂(S) denotes the set of edges joining S to its complement and r is the harmonic mean of the minimum and maximum degrees of X. – Diverse applications of the results are mentioned.\n\n* * *\n\n[Input]  \n\n  * ### On Weighted Sequence Sums\n\n  *   * Yahya Ould Hamidoune\n  * Journal:\n    Combinatorics, Probability and Computing / Volume 4 / Issue 4 / December 1995\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:\n    12 September 2008, pp. 363-367\n  *     * Article\n    *       * Get access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n      *     * Export citation\n  * View abstract\n\nThe main result of this paper has the following consequence. Let G be an abelian group of order n. Let {x i: 1 ≤ 2n − 1} be a family of elements of G and let {w i: 1 ≤ i ≤ n − 1} be a family of integers prime relative to n. Then there is a permutation & of [1,2n − 1] such that\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA weighted generalization of two theorems of Gao (https://www.academia.edu/142988939/A_weighted_generalization_of_two_theorems_of_Gao)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Allowing the weight sequence W to be equal to the length of the sequence S over G, Hamidoune [21] was able to give a weighted version of Gao’s theorem. ... Griffiths, The Erdos-Ginzurg-Ziv theorem with units, Discrete Math., 308 (2008), no. ... Grynkiewicz, On a conjecture of Hamidoune for subsequence sums, Integers, 5 (2005), no.\nAllowing the weight sequence W to be equal to the length of the sequence S over G, Hamidoune [21] was able to give a weighted version of Gao’s theorem. But it is perhaps the weighted variation introduced by Adhikari, et al. [1] [4] that has received the most attention in the past couple years. Here, instead of considering a fixed sequence of weights, a nonempty subset A ⊆ Z of weights is considered. The questions then become, what is the minimum length DA (G) so that every sequence over G of this length contains a subsequence r P s1 · · · sr with ai si = 0 for some ai ∈ A, and what is the minimum length EA (G) so that every i=1 sequence over G of this length contains a subsequence s1 · · · s|G| of length |G| with |G| P ai si = 0 i=1 for some ai ∈ A. Of course, this is equivalent to considering arbitrarily long sequences of weights with fixed support equal to A (and no term having small multiplicity), a view we will adopt to be able to use the same notation from other weighted sequence problems. 2, A7 (electronic). [19] D. J. Grynkiewicz and J. Zhuang, Weighted Sequences in Finite Cyclic Groups, Applied Mathematics E-Notes, 9 (2009), 40–46. [20] D. J. Grynkiewicz, L. E. Marchan and O. Ordaz, Representation of finite abelian group elements by subsequence sums, to appear in J. Théor. Nombres Bordeaux. [21] Y. Hamidoune, A weighted Generalization of Gao’s n + D − 1 Theorem, Combin. Probab. Comput., 17 (2008), no. 6, 1–6. [22] Y. O. Hamidoune, On weighted sequence sums, Comb. Prob. Comput., 4 (1995), 363–367. [23] Y. O. Hamidoune, On weighted sums in abelian groups, Discrete Math., 162 (1996), 127–132. [24] Y. O. Hamidoune, O. Ordaz an